WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.626
- Here's another one.

00:00:01.626 --> 00:00:03.378
Can I be on your podcast?

00:00:03.378 --> 00:00:07.298
- Rich! What? Come on.

00:00:07.298 --> 00:00:10.802
(lofi-jazz music playing)

00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:30.989
- Today we're gonna take some
of life's biggest questions.

00:00:30.989 --> 00:00:33.199
We're gonna toss them in a Vitamix,

00:00:33.199 --> 00:00:35.535
we're gonna press hyper blend,

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:37.996
create a metaphysical milkshake,

00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:41.166
drink it down and nourish the soul.

00:00:41.166 --> 00:00:43.585
My brothers for this mystical journey are

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:45.920
none other than Rainn Wilson.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.590
You know, this guy stars
screens big and small.

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:52.510
Best known for his portrayal
of Dwight Schrute on The Office

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:55.388
and author, producer, TV host,

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:58.975
and scholar of religions, Reza Aslan.

00:00:58.975 --> 00:01:01.352
These two have teamed
up for a podcast called,

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:03.772
you guessed it, metaphysical milkshake,

00:01:03.772 --> 00:01:05.607
which you should all check out.

00:01:05.607 --> 00:01:08.568
I've wanted to meet both
these guys since forever.

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.070
It finally happened.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:13.615
Here we are and it is glorious.

00:01:13.615 --> 00:01:15.158
So hit that subscribe button,

00:01:15.158 --> 00:01:17.744
prepare to have your noodle bent

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:21.664
and enjoy my Vulcan mind
meld with Reza and Rainn.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.834
Super nice to meet you guys.

00:01:24.834 --> 00:01:26.878
I've been wanting to meet
both of you for a long time,

00:01:26.878 --> 00:01:28.004
and it means a lot that
you would come out here

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:30.548
and do this in person with me.

00:01:30.548 --> 00:01:31.800
I love the new podcast.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.051
It's super cool.

00:01:33.051 --> 00:01:35.011
We're gonna get into all of it.

00:01:35.011 --> 00:01:36.638
We're gonna talk about

00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:39.140
the questions that really matter.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.851
But first, before we
do that, I have to say.

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:45.021
Rainn, I loved you on The Choe Show.

00:01:45.021 --> 00:01:46.940
I'm obsessed with The Choe Show.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.191
All things David Choe.

00:01:48.191 --> 00:01:52.195
I loved how you unboxed
the painting the other day.

00:01:52.195 --> 00:01:54.697
And I'm trying to get David on the show.

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:55.532
Like I just...

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:57.033
There's something about that guy.

00:01:57.867 --> 00:01:58.868
It's so magnetizing.

00:01:59.702 --> 00:02:02.664
His earnestness and his childlike nature

00:02:02.664 --> 00:02:05.166
that I just feel like I want
to have that guy in my life-

00:02:05.166 --> 00:02:06.000
- I'm gonna hug this out-

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.794
- How did you become friends?

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.715
- Oh, Joe and I became
friends through mutual...

00:02:12.715 --> 00:02:15.093
Through mutual friends and met.

00:02:17.178 --> 00:02:19.848
And then this guy I was
hosting this kind of retreat,

00:02:19.848 --> 00:02:22.517
this like men's weekend retreat in Hawaii.

00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:26.187
And then we hung out for a
whole weekend in Hawaii and went

00:02:26.187 --> 00:02:29.315
snorkeling and exploring and sharing.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:33.111
And he kind of, Choe shares
everything about his life.

00:02:33.111 --> 00:02:36.030
- Its all on the sleeve with that guy.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.032
Which is so refreshing.

00:02:38.032 --> 00:02:39.325
You know?
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:40.410
Well, he used to not be.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:41.786
He used to be the opposite of that.

00:02:41.786 --> 00:02:45.748
So he used to be jaded and
cynical and withholding

00:02:45.748 --> 00:02:47.125
and too cool for school.

00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:50.170
And that the crazy guy who

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.130
spent months in a Japanese prison,

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:55.592
and now he's on this
journey of self discovery

00:02:55.592 --> 00:02:57.552
and it's really raw.

00:02:57.552 --> 00:02:59.721
And I really appreciate what he does.

00:02:59.721 --> 00:03:02.098
And yeah, he's become a good friend

00:03:02.098 --> 00:03:04.976
and plus admire his incredible heart.

00:03:04.976 --> 00:03:07.478
I mean, his artistry is astonishing.

00:03:07.478 --> 00:03:08.354
- Yeah, I know.

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:10.523
I hope they get a second season of that.

00:03:10.523 --> 00:03:11.858
- [Rainn] Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:12.775 --> 00:03:16.196
- Similarly, I watched
the Bourdain doc recently,

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:17.030
Road Runner.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:17.864
Have you seen it yet?

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:18.907
- Not yet. No.

00:03:18.907 --> 00:03:21.075
- And David plays a big part in that,

00:03:21.075 --> 00:03:23.286
especially near the end.

00:03:23.286 --> 00:03:25.038
But in thinking about that documentary

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:28.666
and I suspect the influence

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:31.211
that Anthony had like on your own life

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:34.839
and how you conceptualize
believer and how you

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:39.260
pursued those stories through that show.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.888
I think there seems to be a
lot of similarities there.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:46.184
- Yeah. Unfortunately, I never
got a chance to meet Tony,

00:03:46.184 --> 00:03:49.896
but I mean, his specter

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:52.690
is at every part of CNN,

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.442
at least it used to be.

00:03:54.442 --> 00:03:55.902
And you know, this was a time in which

00:03:55.902 --> 00:03:57.528
I think CNN was trying to get into

00:03:57.528 --> 00:03:59.155
the entertainment business

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.743
and they're not as much anymore. I think.

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:06.037
It's funny cause I think about

00:04:06.037 --> 00:04:09.999
what they were doing during
that decade and the...

00:04:12.043 --> 00:04:15.588
In so far as marketing and the
entertainment industry goes,

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:16.881
they had this very smart plan,

00:04:16.881 --> 00:04:21.886
which was these Bourdain-like hosts.

00:04:22.011 --> 00:04:24.013
They would get somebody
with strong opinions

00:04:24.013 --> 00:04:27.976
and strong views and then
they would let them pursue

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:28.810
their expertise-

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.145
- Their passion.
- Their passion. Yes, exactly.

00:04:32.397 --> 00:04:35.650
But then there was this tension because

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:38.152
they were a news outfit

00:04:38.152 --> 00:04:41.239
and a aggressively

00:04:41.239 --> 00:04:44.158
center news outfit.

00:04:44.158 --> 00:04:48.162
So there was this
constant conflict between,

00:04:48.162 --> 00:04:51.499
"Let's get people with big opinions

00:04:51.499 --> 00:04:54.711
and big personalities
and let's empower them

00:04:54.711 --> 00:04:58.589
to go out there and show
our viewers the world."

00:04:58.589 --> 00:05:03.094
But also they can't say anything
newsworthy or political or

00:05:03.094 --> 00:05:06.139
- Controversial.
- Controversial or anything.

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:08.975
I mean, Bourdain had a lot to say,

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:11.728
but he was smart enough to
say it about British politics.

00:05:11.728 --> 00:05:13.521
(all laughing)

00:05:13.521 --> 00:05:15.273
- He bit his tongue on American politics.

00:05:15.273 --> 00:05:20.153
- The things this guy would
say about British politicians

00:05:20.153 --> 00:05:22.447
would have definitely
have gotten him fired.

00:05:22.447 --> 00:05:26.200
But I think everybody else
very clearly got the message.

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.453
And now they're just not doing it anymore.

00:05:28.453 --> 00:05:29.412
- Times are different.

00:05:29.412 --> 00:05:31.122
- Now they're like
forget about that whole-

00:05:31.122 --> 00:05:33.875
- They have Stanley Tucci going
around Italy sampling pasta.

00:05:33.875 --> 00:05:35.918
- That's what I mean.
- So its much more milk toast.

00:05:35.918 --> 00:05:37.462
- It's all milk toast now.

00:05:38.421 --> 00:05:43.259
I think the only person who
has survived is Kamau Bell

00:05:43.259 --> 00:05:44.886
and I think partly,

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:49.307
he learned very quickly

00:05:50.391 --> 00:05:52.143
that he should probably just keep his

00:05:52.143 --> 00:05:53.770
political opinions to himself.

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:58.483
I mean, he'll make certain
comments here and there,

00:05:58.483 --> 00:06:02.236
but nothing controversial.
- That kind of programming

00:06:02.236 --> 00:06:04.864
isn't gonna exist at a
place like CNN anymore.

00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:06.824
but there are a multitude of places

00:06:06.824 --> 00:06:08.284
where you can do that kind of thing,

00:06:08.284 --> 00:06:11.162
like Netflix or maybe Vice
or something like that.

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:12.205
- Used to be. Yeah.

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:13.915
I think that's...

00:06:13.915 --> 00:06:17.377
That whole genre has pretty much gone

00:06:17.377 --> 00:06:19.087
by the wayside nowadays.

00:06:19.087 --> 00:06:22.548
It's hard to find shows like that.

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:26.386
Like the Bourdain, go
around the world show.

00:06:26.386 --> 00:06:30.181
Again, Tucci. It's like
an Italian American

00:06:30.181 --> 00:06:32.225
eating pasta in Italy.

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:36.896
There's nothing controversial or on edge

00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:38.648
about it at all.

00:06:38.648 --> 00:06:41.442
Bourdain would go to Palestine.

00:06:41.442 --> 00:06:42.652
He would go to Iran.

00:06:42.652 --> 00:06:47.073
- Yeah, yeah. And Haiti and
talk about income inequality.

00:06:48.533 --> 00:06:49.534
- Aint nobody doing that.

00:06:49.534 --> 00:06:51.661
- Yeah. He was in Beirut when

00:06:51.661 --> 00:06:54.122
all kinds of craziness broke out there.

00:06:54.122 --> 00:06:57.750
- It's just funny cause
they could have done,

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.252
they could've just put a wall,

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:01.671
like the way Fox news
pretends to do, right?

00:07:01.671 --> 00:07:06.092
They put a wall between
everything after 8:00 PM

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:09.387
and everything before 8:00
PM or 7:00 PM or whatever.

00:07:09.387 --> 00:07:10.513
- Here, this is opinion.

00:07:10.513 --> 00:07:12.223
- And everything before 7:00 PM is news.

00:07:12.223 --> 00:07:15.101
Anything that happens after
8:00 PM is entertainment.

00:07:15.101 --> 00:07:16.727
It's not even an opinion, right?

00:07:16.727 --> 00:07:17.979
It's just entertainment.

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:21.399
And I think that's what CNN tried to do,

00:07:21.399 --> 00:07:22.525
but they just,

00:07:22.525 --> 00:07:23.359
they didn't go well.

00:07:23.359 --> 00:07:25.194
- I'm actually trying to
get a show off the ground.

00:07:25.194 --> 00:07:26.737
We're actually in negotiations right now,

00:07:26.737 --> 00:07:28.281
I can't really name with who.

00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:30.867
But me going around the world.

00:07:31.826 --> 00:07:33.911
It's called The Geography Of Bliss.

00:07:33.911 --> 00:07:36.747
And it's about what makes
people happy around the world.

00:07:36.747 --> 00:07:37.665
- I love that.

00:07:37.665 --> 00:07:39.625
- So, why are people,

00:07:39.625 --> 00:07:41.669
year after year, happier and Finland

00:07:41.669 --> 00:07:44.338
than they are in Romania?

00:07:44.338 --> 00:07:48.384
And it's not just income, necessarily.

00:07:48.384 --> 00:07:52.847
What is it culturally or
what habits or practices

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:57.268
make people culturally have
a greater sense of wellbeing?

00:07:58.186 --> 00:08:00.396
- I'm sure you're familiar
with Dan Buettner then.

00:08:00.396 --> 00:08:02.231
The Blue Zones guy?

00:08:02.231 --> 00:08:03.399
- Oh yeah. The Blue Zones show.

00:08:03.399 --> 00:08:04.525
- You should have him on your show

00:08:04.525 --> 00:08:06.027
because he's all about that.

00:08:06.027 --> 00:08:07.612
And wrote a whole book about that.

00:08:07.612 --> 00:08:10.114
He has the blue zones of longevity,

00:08:10.114 --> 00:08:11.491
where people live the longest,

00:08:11.491 --> 00:08:13.743
but he's also done a similar study

00:08:13.743 --> 00:08:16.287
to identify the pockets around the world

00:08:16.287 --> 00:08:18.623
where people are the happiest.

00:08:18.623 --> 00:08:20.833
And what makes them happy
and it's counterintuitive.

00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:23.169
It's not the things that we've been taught

00:08:23.169 --> 00:08:27.089
that is shoved down our throats
through marketing and media.

00:08:27.089 --> 00:08:30.551
- Because I was reading this
article speaking of Finland

00:08:30.551 --> 00:08:35.056
and there's a Finnish word
that I don't remember,

00:08:35.056 --> 00:08:38.726
but it basically is a
word that is kind of like,

00:08:38.726 --> 00:08:40.853
essentially translates to

00:08:40.853 --> 00:08:43.898
"Keep your expectations
relatively low and in check."

00:08:45.066 --> 00:08:46.901
So why are they happier?

00:08:46.901 --> 00:08:49.612
Because they're not expecting to live

00:08:49.612 --> 00:08:51.614
a wildly outrageously happy life.

00:08:51.614 --> 00:08:53.574
Whereas we Americans feel like,

00:08:53.574 --> 00:08:55.159
"Life, liberty, pursuit of happiness."

00:08:55.159 --> 00:08:56.452
And it's kind of like,

00:08:56.452 --> 00:08:58.287
"I've got stuff, I've got cotton candy,

00:08:58.287 --> 00:09:02.542
I've got video games, I've
got 147 apps on my phone.

00:09:02.542 --> 00:09:04.001
Why am I not happy?

00:09:04.001 --> 00:09:06.128
I want to be fucking
happy. Why am I not happy?"

00:09:06.128 --> 00:09:11.092
And there's this crazed outrage
for happiness whereas this,

00:09:11.092 --> 00:09:13.010
I'm not sure that the
Finnish way is the right way,

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:15.721
by the way but it was
an interesting article

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:20.726
that had this cultural
tempered response to life.

00:09:21.727 --> 00:09:22.895
So then you're kind of like,

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:25.439
when you are happy you're
pleasantly surprised like,

00:09:25.439 --> 00:09:28.776
"Wow, I ate some boiled fish
and I was a little bit happy.

00:09:28.776 --> 00:09:30.444
Yay me." You know?

00:09:30.444 --> 00:09:33.322
- As opposed to the expectation
that we're meant to be happy

00:09:33.322 --> 00:09:34.323
all the time.

00:09:34.323 --> 00:09:35.199
- Well I would say that-

00:09:35.199 --> 00:09:37.118
- And then suffer disappointment.

00:09:37.118 --> 00:09:39.954
- Baked into the concept of
the American dream, right?

00:09:39.954 --> 00:09:44.333
Is this idea that you can
be more, you can do more,

00:09:44.333 --> 00:09:47.628
your children will be
better off than you are.

00:09:48.629 --> 00:09:52.925
Anyone here can rise to the
heights of wealth and power

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:54.844
with just hard work.

00:09:54.844 --> 00:09:56.345
None of that is true.

00:09:56.345 --> 00:09:58.973
None of that is true
anywhere in the world. Right?

00:09:58.973 --> 00:10:00.641
You could work as hard as you want to.

00:10:00.641 --> 00:10:03.394
You could do everything right.

00:10:03.394 --> 00:10:07.231
And it's just not guaranteed
that there will be a

00:10:07.231 --> 00:10:10.443
steady progress upward on the ladder,

00:10:10.443 --> 00:10:14.614
but imagine living and
being born in a country

00:10:14.614 --> 00:10:17.241
in which that's the ethos.

00:10:17.241 --> 00:10:19.910
That there's more.

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:20.745
There's something else.

00:10:20.745 --> 00:10:23.956
- That there's something
if you haven't achieved it.

00:10:23.956 --> 00:10:25.958
So if you are not better
off than your parents,

00:10:25.958 --> 00:10:27.335
are not better off than your grandparents

00:10:27.335 --> 00:10:30.379
and not happy or making more
money, owning more property,

00:10:30.379 --> 00:10:31.422
it's your fault.

00:10:31.422 --> 00:10:32.548
- Something has gone off the rails.

00:10:32.548 --> 00:10:35.676
With a light dusting of
entitlement on top of it.

00:10:35.676 --> 00:10:37.386
- But the only reason that this did work

00:10:37.386 --> 00:10:38.888
in the United States is because

00:10:38.888 --> 00:10:41.641
we stole a ton of land from the natives

00:10:41.641 --> 00:10:44.560
and we had slave labor to
build our infrastructure.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:46.854
So yeah, from the late 1800 to the,

00:10:46.854 --> 00:10:48.939
let's say 1970s or 1980s,

00:10:48.939 --> 00:10:52.485
there was unchecked growth
because we weren't Finland.

00:10:52.485 --> 00:10:54.862
We weren't an enclosed land area

00:10:54.862 --> 00:10:57.198
just trying to subsist in a land.

00:10:57.198 --> 00:10:59.992
We were just growing and
moving and having railroads

00:10:59.992 --> 00:11:04.455
and having more timber and
gold and natural resources.

00:11:04.455 --> 00:11:06.749
So we were able to kind
of sustain this growth

00:11:06.749 --> 00:11:07.667
through that time period.

00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:09.585
But now, of course, here we are.

00:11:09.585 --> 00:11:11.212
Life, liberty, and the
pursuit of happiness.

00:11:11.212 --> 00:11:13.881
And then for the last generation or two

00:11:13.881 --> 00:11:17.051
things have been getting
worse on every level.

00:11:17.051 --> 00:11:20.680
- Yeah. And philosophically speaking,

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:23.349
a lot of the problems
that we have as a country,

00:11:23.349 --> 00:11:27.311
certainly when it comes to
this, sort of, giant chasm

00:11:27.311 --> 00:11:29.230
in social incomes and

00:11:31.399 --> 00:11:35.945
the huge gap between the
poor and the wealthy.

00:11:35.945 --> 00:11:37.571
The way that we treat wealthy people,

00:11:37.571 --> 00:11:39.907
the way that we think about
religion and spirituality

00:11:39.907 --> 00:11:41.325
in the United States.

00:11:41.325 --> 00:11:46.038
It's all a by-product of this
American Dream ethos, right?

00:11:46.038 --> 00:11:49.750
We look at rich people and
assume that they are rich

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:52.795
because they deserved to be rich. Right?

00:11:52.795 --> 00:11:54.130
We had this conversation a lot

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:55.923
when Trump was running for office, right?

00:11:55.923 --> 00:12:00.928
It's like, "I'm rich so therefore
I know what I am doing."

00:12:01.137 --> 00:12:03.264
We actually did a podcast episode on this,

00:12:03.264 --> 00:12:05.182
this idea that wealth

00:12:06.058 --> 00:12:09.061
doesn't just equal success,

00:12:09.061 --> 00:12:11.397
it equals morality.

00:12:11.397 --> 00:12:15.943
We look at rich people
as being more moral.

00:12:15.943 --> 00:12:17.361
Look at the prosperity gospel,

00:12:17.361 --> 00:12:21.115
a thing that could really only
exist in the United States.

00:12:21.115 --> 00:12:21.949
Right?

00:12:21.949 --> 00:12:23.868
The whole concept of which is

00:12:23.868 --> 00:12:28.122
wealth is indicative of your salvation.

00:12:28.122 --> 00:12:32.918
If you are rich, it's
because God has rewarded you.

00:12:32.918 --> 00:12:37.923
It's a symbol of the
fact that you are saved.

00:12:38.507 --> 00:12:41.302
And so you should pursue wealth

00:12:41.302 --> 00:12:45.055
the way that you would
pursue spiritual edification.

00:12:45.055 --> 00:12:46.807
I mean, name another country

00:12:46.807 --> 00:12:50.186
in which that is a prevailing view of

00:12:50.186 --> 00:12:53.856
tens of millions of the citizenship.

00:12:53.856 --> 00:12:54.774
It's just crazy.

00:12:54.774 --> 00:12:56.400
- Yeah. I think on top of that,

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:57.985
also just the mere statement,

00:12:57.985 --> 00:13:01.697
"The pursuit of happiness" is
wrongheaded in the sense that

00:13:01.697 --> 00:13:05.993
happiness is a byproduct
of other pursuits,

00:13:05.993 --> 00:13:08.579
such as trying to find
purpose in your life.

00:13:08.579 --> 00:13:10.206
Like you mentioned the Finnish word,

00:13:10.206 --> 00:13:12.458
in Okinawa they have a word Ikigai

00:13:12.458 --> 00:13:14.752
which is essentially means

00:13:14.752 --> 00:13:17.004
to live a life that is purposeful.

00:13:17.004 --> 00:13:19.799
To wake up in the morning
and feel a sense of

00:13:19.799 --> 00:13:23.385
purpose and obligation and
happiness being a by-product of

00:13:23.385 --> 00:13:27.223
orienting your life
around that type of ethos,

00:13:27.223 --> 00:13:31.519
rather than the pursuit of
accumulation that has led to

00:13:31.519 --> 00:13:33.562
you know, and this is
what I want to get into,

00:13:33.562 --> 00:13:37.316
this crisis of consciousness
that we find ourselves.

00:13:37.316 --> 00:13:40.778
Certainly, religion is a

00:13:40.778 --> 00:13:42.655
predominant aspect of

00:13:42.655 --> 00:13:45.783
living in America but
on a spiritual level,

00:13:45.783 --> 00:13:48.702
I feel like we're bereft

00:13:50.955 --> 00:13:53.082
of the meaning that is behind

00:13:53.082 --> 00:13:54.875
the pursuit of a spiritual life

00:13:54.875 --> 00:13:59.171
and that's led to so much
of our fractured society

00:13:59.171 --> 00:14:01.632
and our inability to
communicate with each other

00:14:01.632 --> 00:14:04.593
and the way in which we
prioritize our lives.

00:14:05.970 --> 00:14:07.096
- We're all about that, man.

00:14:07.096 --> 00:14:10.224
- Yeah, I know. I know, this is your jam.

00:14:10.224 --> 00:14:12.935
This is why, a big reason why I wanted

00:14:12.935 --> 00:14:13.769
to have you guys here.

00:14:13.769 --> 00:14:16.605
Like I'm looking at the
people that you've selected

00:14:16.605 --> 00:14:17.481
to have on your show.

00:14:17.481 --> 00:14:19.608
There's an overlap with some of my guests

00:14:19.608 --> 00:14:20.943
and I know you have some upcoming guests

00:14:20.943 --> 00:14:23.070
that are people that I've had on my show.

00:14:23.946 --> 00:14:25.072
And you know the...

00:14:25.072 --> 00:14:27.408
- We basically looked
through your guest lists

00:14:27.408 --> 00:14:30.327
and we thought, "We gotta
get that Rabbi Mordecai."

