00:24:42.647 --> 00:24:44.816
with what we have here in front of us,

00:24:44.816 --> 00:24:46.902
which is essentially a fishbowl with

00:24:46.902 --> 00:24:48.403
much of wadded up pieces of
paper on each piece of paper.

00:24:48.403 --> 00:24:50.238
On each piece of paper here

00:24:50.238 --> 00:24:53.283
I put one of these big questions.

00:24:53.283 --> 00:24:55.535
So I thought we could just pick one out.

00:24:55.535 --> 00:24:57.245
This could go terribly wrong, by the way,

00:24:57.245 --> 00:24:59.414
this could be as awful as (indistinct).

00:24:59.414 --> 00:25:01.249
and have the dorm room conversation.

00:25:01.249 --> 00:25:02.125
- Lets do it. Lets pretend-

00:25:02.125 --> 00:25:02.959
- [Rainn] Pretend we're in conversation.

00:25:02.959 --> 00:25:04.961
- Lets pretend were freshmen.

00:25:04.961 --> 00:25:06.630
First time away from out parents.

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:08.715
- And if we've already
talked about it or it's bad,

00:25:08.715 --> 00:25:11.009
we could just go back to the well.

00:25:11.009 --> 00:25:11.843
- What you got there?

00:25:11.843 --> 00:25:13.845
- There's some stupid ones in there too.

00:25:13.845 --> 00:25:15.472
- Does everybody have a purpose?

00:25:15.472 --> 00:25:16.431
- [Rich] That's a good one.

00:25:16.431 --> 00:25:17.516
- [Rainn] I think that's a great one.

00:25:17.516 --> 00:25:21.978
- Let's do that one. Let's do that one.

00:25:24.523 --> 00:25:26.775
- I believe that everyone has a purpose.

00:25:26.775 --> 00:25:29.319
I believe this with all of my heart

00:25:29.319 --> 00:25:33.323
and in a very strong way because

00:25:34.574 --> 00:25:36.785
as a member of the Baha'i faith

00:25:36.785 --> 00:25:38.036
but not just the Baha'i faith,

00:25:38.036 --> 00:25:40.497
I think anyone who has...

00:25:40.497 --> 00:25:42.541
I mean, I think that's an
answer that applies to both

00:25:42.541 --> 00:25:44.709
theists and atheists,

00:25:44.709 --> 00:25:47.796
but as a member of the
Bahai faith, I believe that

00:25:48.922 --> 00:25:51.424
we are on an eternal spiritual journey.

00:25:51.424 --> 00:25:56.263
That this, you know, our soul,
our eternal part of ourselves

00:25:56.263 --> 00:26:00.475
is encased in this flesh
tuxedo for 80 or 90 years.

00:26:00.475 --> 00:26:02.811
We slough it off and
we continue our journey

00:26:02.811 --> 00:26:04.646
beyond time and space.

00:26:04.646 --> 00:26:08.567
So while we're here,
everyone has a purpose.

00:26:08.567 --> 00:26:12.404
Now it may not be everyone
on planet earth has a purpose

00:26:12.404 --> 00:26:15.198
like Oprah has a purpose.

00:26:16.241 --> 00:26:18.410
- We can't all be Oprahs.

00:26:18.410 --> 00:26:20.370
- We can't have 7 billion
Oprah's on the planet.

00:26:20.370 --> 00:26:21.705
But I do think that

00:26:22.872 --> 00:26:27.043
this is one of the really exciting tasks

00:26:28.962 --> 00:26:31.923
is there's there's a question wrapped,

00:26:31.923 --> 00:26:33.967
there's a riddle wrapped
inside this question,

00:26:33.967 --> 00:26:37.804
which is our purpose
is to find our purpose.

00:26:37.804 --> 00:26:39.848
And as soon as we found our purpose,

00:26:39.848 --> 00:26:41.600
we have to be not sure
that that's our purpose

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:43.059
and we have to keep finding our purpose.

00:26:43.059 --> 00:26:46.479
So by the time we're 97 and we keel over,

00:26:46.479 --> 00:26:49.941
hopefully we're all
continuously on this...

00:26:52.027 --> 00:26:55.030
At the very beginning footsteps
of this eternal journey

00:26:55.030 --> 00:26:57.782
toward finding what that purpose is

00:26:57.782 --> 00:26:59.951
and it may shift and change.

00:26:59.951 --> 00:27:03.622
And so whether you call that God

00:27:03.622 --> 00:27:07.042
or The Great Spirit or just
the power of the universe

00:27:07.042 --> 00:27:10.670
or the winds of the cosmos
or the mystic connection

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:12.756
or whatever you want to call God,

00:27:12.756 --> 00:27:14.174
which has become a four letter word

00:27:14.174 --> 00:27:16.926
and is a tough concept to dig into,

00:27:16.926 --> 00:27:18.928
but the winds, the holy spirit,

00:27:18.928 --> 00:27:21.931
the winds of the cosmic
power of the creative force

00:27:21.931 --> 00:27:24.392
that surrounds us, that's
within us, without us,

00:27:24.392 --> 00:27:27.270
beyond us, beyond limitations
and time and space.

00:27:29.105 --> 00:27:32.275
These winds have a very special...

00:27:34.069 --> 00:27:36.905
We have a very special
relationship with them, I believe.

00:27:36.905 --> 00:27:37.739
And it's for...

00:27:38.782 --> 00:27:40.367
It's like when you go sailing,

00:27:40.367 --> 00:27:42.077
I don't know anything about sailing.

00:27:42.077 --> 00:27:43.328
I've been sailing like twice,

00:27:43.328 --> 00:27:46.623
but the whole idea that
you tack your sails to try

00:27:46.623 --> 00:27:49.125
and get the most out of
the wind depending on,

00:27:49.125 --> 00:27:49.959
you have a vague, you know,

00:27:49.959 --> 00:27:51.252
"I want to go toward that island,"

00:27:51.252 --> 00:27:53.380
but you may not go directly to the island.

00:27:53.380 --> 00:27:55.340
The winds may take you off
to the left a little bit,

00:27:55.340 --> 00:27:57.425
and then back, or you may have to zigzag.

00:27:57.425 --> 00:28:00.303
The wind stall for a little
bit. You turn on your motor.

00:28:00.303 --> 00:28:01.680
Then the winds are
pushing you right there,

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:06.267
but you are trying to align
yourself with those winds.

00:28:06.267 --> 00:28:09.020
And it's really, it's exciting.

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:12.148
I think it's a really
exciting part of being alive.

00:28:12.148 --> 00:28:14.734
Maybe the most exciting
part of being alive.

00:28:14.734 --> 00:28:18.863
And this is where I think
that young people, especially,

00:28:18.863 --> 00:28:20.198
I don't know about older people.

00:28:20.198 --> 00:28:22.158
I mean, older people,
we get set in our ways

00:28:22.158 --> 00:28:24.744
and like Reza said, we've got
kids and jobs and mortgages,

00:28:24.744 --> 00:28:27.914
and we have a little bit
of stuff figured out.

00:28:27.914 --> 00:28:30.792
But especially for this
mental health crisis

00:28:30.792 --> 00:28:34.587
that is afflicting kids under 20 or 30,

00:28:35.755 --> 00:28:40.635
it's astonishingly insidious
and toxic and fatal.

00:28:41.803 --> 00:28:45.640
But this connection to this question

00:28:46.725 --> 00:28:48.351
is a big part of

00:28:48.351 --> 00:28:52.689
helping to use a spiritual
tool to solve that issue,

00:28:52.689 --> 00:28:53.648
solve that problem.

00:28:53.648 --> 00:28:54.691
- Beautifully put.

00:28:54.691 --> 00:28:55.525
- Hard agree.

00:28:55.525 --> 00:28:56.484
Thanks. Can we just end the podcast there?

00:28:56.484 --> 00:28:57.777
- Hard agree.
- yeah boom.

00:28:57.777 --> 00:29:00.363
- The only thing that
I'll just say to people

00:29:00.363 --> 00:29:01.698
who are listening to this,

00:29:01.698 --> 00:29:05.618
because I could listen to that all day.

00:29:05.618 --> 00:29:07.203
It's a hundred percent true.

00:29:07.203 --> 00:29:10.248
I agree with it a hundred percent

00:29:10.248 --> 00:29:13.001
and I have like philosophical
and theological reasons why

00:29:13.001 --> 00:29:14.043
I agree with it

00:29:14.043 --> 00:29:15.920
and experiential reasons
what I agree with it.

00:29:15.920 --> 00:29:17.422
But what I'll just say is that

00:29:18.506 --> 00:29:19.924
if you are listening to this and saying,

00:29:19.924 --> 00:29:21.634
"Okay, fine. Everybody has a purpose.

00:29:21.634 --> 00:29:24.512
Will then, you know, how do I...

00:29:24.512 --> 00:29:25.555
Am I doing my purpose?

00:29:25.555 --> 00:29:27.557
How do I know what my purpose is?"

00:29:28.892 --> 00:29:31.895
If you don't know, then
you're not doing it.

00:29:34.105 --> 00:29:35.690
For those people...

00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:38.902
I feel like I am achieving my purpose.

00:29:38.902 --> 00:29:41.821
I would say probably you
too, Rich. Yeah, Rainn.

00:29:41.821 --> 00:29:45.116
I mean, you're doing what your purpose is.

00:29:45.116 --> 00:29:46.534
- I wrestle with it.

00:29:46.534 --> 00:29:49.954
Truth be told. but yes,
by and large, I do.

00:29:49.954 --> 00:29:50.789
- [Reza] Well, I mean-

00:29:50.789 --> 00:29:54.083
- It's a constant, for me
a constant a series of the,

00:29:54.083 --> 00:29:55.919
we use going back to
the sailboat metaphor,

00:29:55.919 --> 00:29:57.128
adjusting those sails.

00:29:58.087 --> 00:30:00.215
- That metaphor is so perfect

00:30:00.215 --> 00:30:04.302
because everyone who has had
this experience will tell you

00:30:04.302 --> 00:30:09.140
that when the wind is
right and your sail is

00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:10.850
exactly where it's supposed to be,

00:30:10.850 --> 00:30:12.894
and you're moving smoothly.

00:30:12.894 --> 00:30:15.188
And I mean this metaphorically
not actually on the water,

00:30:15.188 --> 00:30:19.108
but I mean in life, you
know it. You know it.

00:30:19.108 --> 00:30:21.903
And you always know when
you're fighting the wind.

00:30:23.196 --> 00:30:25.782
So pay attention to that feeling.

00:30:25.782 --> 00:30:27.575
Pay attention that. If you
sitting there thinking yourself,

00:30:27.575 --> 00:30:30.787
"Well, then how do I
know what my purpose is?"

00:30:30.787 --> 00:30:32.789
Are you fighting the wind?

00:30:32.789 --> 00:30:34.332
Is that what it feels like?

00:30:34.332 --> 00:30:37.001
Then you're not pursuing your purpose.

00:30:37.001 --> 00:30:39.462
- And here's another thing I'll say.

00:30:39.462 --> 00:30:40.421
You ready for this?

00:30:40.421 --> 00:30:42.465
- I'm ready. The bar is high now.

00:30:42.465 --> 00:30:43.466
- Okay.
(Reza laughs)

00:30:43.466 --> 00:30:45.635
Fasten your podcast seat belts, Rich.

00:30:48.429 --> 00:30:50.431
If people are listening
to this and saying,

00:30:50.431 --> 00:30:52.058
"Well, I just don't know.

00:30:52.058 --> 00:30:55.061
I can't find mine and I've
tried to be a massage therapist

00:30:55.061 --> 00:30:56.563
and then I had a job as this,

00:30:56.563 --> 00:30:58.815
and I tried to start up a business here

00:30:58.815 --> 00:31:00.149
and I went back to school for this,

00:31:00.149 --> 00:31:02.527
and it's not working out and wah, wah."

00:31:02.527 --> 00:31:04.070
Well, I will say,

00:31:04.070 --> 00:31:05.697
and this is gonna sound crazy.

00:31:05.697 --> 00:31:08.408
It's gonna sound crazy. Pray.

00:31:09.367 --> 00:31:13.329
Pray. That doesn't mean
you have to go into

00:31:13.329 --> 00:31:16.040
a Catholic church and cross yourself

00:31:16.040 --> 00:31:17.667
and look at an icon to pray.

00:31:17.667 --> 00:31:19.836
It doesn't mean that there's
an old white man with a beard

00:31:19.836 --> 00:31:23.923
on a cloud granting wishes
like Santa Claus from up above.

00:31:25.091 --> 00:31:27.677
But we live in a society, in LA at least,

00:31:27.677 --> 00:31:30.555
and I find this really interesting that

00:31:30.555 --> 00:31:33.224
everyone in LA, not everyone,
a lot of people in LA

00:31:33.224 --> 00:31:35.393
meditate but don't pray

00:31:35.393 --> 00:31:37.854
Because "I have such a hard
time with the higher power"

00:31:37.854 --> 00:31:40.982
and everyone in mid America
prays and doesn't meditate.

00:31:41.983 --> 00:31:44.652
So they don't deeply contemplate,

00:31:44.652 --> 00:31:48.781
or they're not open to
receiving the wisdom

00:31:48.781 --> 00:31:51.492
from the universe, from the act of prayer.

00:31:51.492 --> 00:31:54.495
But people in LA are just
receiving and never asking,

00:31:54.495 --> 00:31:55.872
because another reason why,

00:31:55.872 --> 00:31:58.333
because it takes humility to pray

00:31:58.333 --> 00:32:00.960
and people in LA, on the coast,

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:02.086
don't have a lot of humility.

00:32:02.086 --> 00:32:03.421
It's in short supply.

00:32:03.421 --> 00:32:05.214
When you pray, and again,

00:32:05.214 --> 00:32:08.176
I'm just talking about opening
your heart to the spirit

00:32:08.176 --> 00:32:09.636
of the cosmic creative
force of the universe.

00:32:09.636 --> 00:32:11.137
I'm not talking about-

00:32:11.137 --> 00:32:11.971
- Crossing yourself in a...

00:32:11.971 --> 00:32:14.766
- "Oh God, please give me a pony.

00:32:14.766 --> 00:32:19.103
It's just saying, "Universe,
where would you have me go?"

00:32:19.103 --> 00:32:21.648
"All loving Providence that surrounds me,

00:32:21.648 --> 00:32:25.360
what would you have me do and
how would you have me do it?

00:32:25.360 --> 00:32:27.236
I'm open to signs."

00:32:27.236 --> 00:32:28.988
Just be like, "I'm open."

00:32:28.988 --> 00:32:31.366
You can just wake up and
you just say, "I'm open"

00:32:31.366 --> 00:32:32.659
and that's a prayer.

00:32:32.659 --> 00:32:34.953
- Yeah. There's an expansiveness to

00:32:34.953 --> 00:32:37.538
broadening your perception and

00:32:37.538 --> 00:32:41.042
your willingness to just
be in that receiving mode

00:32:41.042 --> 00:32:42.210
and in the asking mode.

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:45.254
But I think we all, to some extent,

00:32:45.254 --> 00:32:47.465
have this yearning inside of us.

00:32:47.465 --> 00:32:49.384
For some, they're very conscious of it,

00:32:49.384 --> 00:32:52.220
for others, perhaps it's
a little bit more muted,

00:32:52.220 --> 00:32:55.932
but this sense of there is
something for me out there.

00:32:55.932 --> 00:32:58.768
And I do think there's
beauty in pursuing that,

00:32:58.768 --> 00:33:00.603
but I think you're right, Reza,

00:33:00.603 --> 00:33:03.648
most people don't know
what their purpose is.

00:33:03.648 --> 00:33:05.191
Perhaps they don't spend enough time

00:33:05.191 --> 00:33:07.860
or very much time at
all thinking about it.

00:33:07.860 --> 00:33:10.863
But I do believe that it
is out there for everybody.

00:33:10.863 --> 00:33:13.783
And I think that part
of the beauty of life

00:33:13.783 --> 00:33:15.535
is the pursuit of finding that.

00:33:15.535 --> 00:33:18.413
And I would tweak a little
bit Rainn what you said,

00:33:18.413 --> 00:33:19.831
this idea that once you find it

00:33:19.831 --> 00:33:21.082
then you continue to find it.

00:33:21.082 --> 00:33:24.377
Like you iterate on it or you
remain open to it changing

00:33:24.377 --> 00:33:26.379
or morphing in certain ways.

00:33:26.379 --> 00:33:29.257
But I think an important
piece also to that is

00:33:29.257 --> 00:33:32.135
then sharing what you learned
in service to other people.

00:33:32.135 --> 00:33:34.887
The service piece of it I think really

00:33:34.887 --> 00:33:37.974
strengthens that sense of purpose

00:33:37.974 --> 00:33:41.436
and the search for it is,

00:33:41.436 --> 00:33:43.479
yes, prayer, meditation and all of that

00:33:43.479 --> 00:33:45.732
but ultimately it is an inside job.

00:33:45.732 --> 00:33:46.941
You have to become

00:33:48.401 --> 00:33:49.944
integrated with who you are.

00:33:49.944 --> 00:33:53.114
You have to get to know
yourself, like know thyself,

00:33:53.114 --> 00:33:56.534
because otherwise you're not
gonna hear that vibration

00:33:56.534 --> 00:34:00.538
and your boat is gonna be
tacking against the wind.

00:34:00.538 --> 00:34:03.499
And it's only when you
know yourself well enough

00:34:03.499 --> 00:34:07.045
that you can determine, "Okay,
I have to course correct

00:34:07.045 --> 00:34:11.215
and here's how I get more in
alignment with like, my energy

00:34:11.215 --> 00:34:13.718
and paying attention
to the way the universe

00:34:13.718 --> 00:34:14.635
responds to that."

00:34:14.635 --> 00:34:17.555
So you can make those
fine tuned adjustments

00:34:17.555 --> 00:34:21.184
that lead you in that
direction that you seek.

00:34:21.184 --> 00:34:23.811
- I mean, I'll use myself as an example.

00:34:23.811 --> 00:34:25.354
Classic LA narcissist.

00:34:27.065 --> 00:34:28.649
- A Praying narcissists.

00:34:28.649 --> 00:34:29.984
(Rainn laughs)

00:34:29.984 --> 00:34:33.529
- He prays to himself. He
should have mentioned that part.

00:34:33.529 --> 00:34:34.530
- To my bobble head.

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:38.117
(Reza and Rich laughing)

00:34:38.117 --> 00:34:42.080
For most of my life, my
purpose was to be an actor.

00:34:42.080 --> 00:34:43.331
That's all I wanted to do.

00:34:43.331 --> 00:34:44.832
I just wanted to learn how to act.

00:34:44.832 --> 00:34:47.001
I wanted to be a better and better actor.

00:34:47.001 --> 00:34:48.711
I wanted to learn skills of acting.

00:34:48.711 --> 00:34:50.755
Then I wanted to learn how
to act in front of a camera

00:34:50.755 --> 00:34:52.673
and I wanted to do comedy and drama

00:34:52.673 --> 00:34:54.509
and play different kinds of roles

00:34:54.509 --> 00:34:58.638
and play serial killers
and weirdos and slapstick

00:34:58.638 --> 00:34:59.931
and you name it.

00:34:59.931 --> 00:35:02.934
And my whole life from
when I was a teenager

00:35:02.934 --> 00:35:05.061
and I first started acting at 16,

00:35:06.145 --> 00:35:10.525
all the way up well into my
forties and late forties,

00:35:10.525 --> 00:35:14.070
I lived, slept and breathed acting.

00:35:14.070 --> 00:35:17.740
And I saw myself as actor, as storyteller.

00:35:17.740 --> 00:35:19.784
I didn't just say it like,
"Oh, I just want a job

00:35:19.784 --> 00:35:20.618
so I can buy a house."

00:35:20.618 --> 00:35:22.286
Although that was nice.

00:35:22.286 --> 00:35:25.581
But you know, as an actor
in the greatest sense

00:35:25.581 --> 00:35:28.042
you're part of telling
a story and entertaining

00:35:28.042 --> 00:35:30.128
and there is a service component to it.

00:35:31.420 --> 00:35:33.005
And then it's been very interesting for me

00:35:33.005 --> 00:35:36.676
because over the last
six, eight years or so,

00:35:36.676 --> 00:35:41.514
it's held much less, kind of,

00:35:41.514 --> 00:35:43.015
of a draw.

00:35:43.015 --> 00:35:45.017
I still like doing it sometimes, but

00:35:47.645 --> 00:35:50.982
my life isn't based around
that next acting job.

00:35:50.982 --> 00:35:54.068
So it has been like, "Is
this a change in purpose?

00:35:54.068 --> 00:35:55.778
A shift in purpose in my life.?

00:35:55.778 --> 00:36:00.074
So starting SoulPancake,
writing more stuff,

00:36:00.074 --> 00:36:03.161
writing books, working
with Reza like on this pod

00:36:03.161 --> 00:36:06.038
and doing more service
work, nonprofit work

00:36:06.038 --> 00:36:08.624
that I do with my wife
in Haiti, et cetera.

00:36:08.624 --> 00:36:11.627
Finding more meaning from that stuff

00:36:11.627 --> 00:36:13.171
cause purposes can change.

00:36:13.171 --> 00:36:14.547
- Yeah. That's interesting.

00:36:14.547 --> 00:36:18.467
I mean, you're a G in the
whole digital content space.

00:36:18.467 --> 00:36:21.762
I mean, SoulPancake was 2009.

00:36:21.762 --> 00:36:23.431
When did you start that?
- Yeah.

00:36:23.431 --> 00:36:27.018
- Yeah. I mean, pretty early
in the whole high-vibe,

00:36:27.018 --> 00:36:28.978
sharing, cool content.

00:36:30.104 --> 00:36:30.938
- We were the first.

00:36:30.938 --> 00:36:33.816
- Taking wisdom and making
it cool for young people.

00:36:33.816 --> 00:36:35.651
Creating digestible content.

00:36:35.651 --> 00:36:38.196
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:36:38.196 --> 00:36:42.617
It was an amazing adventure to
start that with some friends

00:36:42.617 --> 00:36:44.744
and to just be ideating on like,

00:36:44.744 --> 00:36:46.954
speaking of that service component,

00:36:46.954 --> 00:36:48.706
what can we do for young
people on the internet

00:36:48.706 --> 00:36:51.417
that's cool and fun and
that can be a business?

00:36:51.417 --> 00:36:53.669
That can be a sustainable
business model, as well.

00:36:53.669 --> 00:36:57.131
- Wasn't Metaphysical
Milkshake in the back of a van

00:36:57.131 --> 00:36:59.425
or something, or did you
have a different name then?

00:36:59.425 --> 00:37:00.468
You were kind of doing-

00:37:00.468 --> 00:37:02.970
- We used to drive around
in a van and asked people to

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:06.098
get in and talk about existential issue.

00:37:07.516 --> 00:37:08.893
- [Rich] Steve-O's doing that now.

00:37:08.893 --> 00:37:10.645
(all laugh)

00:37:10.645 --> 00:37:13.189
- The, I guess....

00:37:14.482 --> 00:37:17.193
Metaphysical Milkshake was the
original name of SoulPancake.

00:37:17.193 --> 00:37:18.945
The very first name.

00:37:18.945 --> 00:37:21.614
but we quickly scrapped
that because it was so weird

00:37:21.614 --> 00:37:23.157
and it became,

00:37:23.157 --> 00:37:24.492
yeah, it was a talk show
in the back of my van.

00:37:24.492 --> 00:37:26.577
But it was about life's big questions,

00:37:26.577 --> 00:37:29.830
but I would interview
actors and celebrities

00:37:29.830 --> 00:37:30.665
and stuff like that.

00:37:30.665 --> 00:37:32.833
So we just kind of held onto the name.

00:37:32.833 --> 00:37:36.128
I love the name and moved
it into the podcast space.

00:37:36.128 --> 00:37:37.338
- Reza, you were about to say something.

00:37:37.338 --> 00:37:42.301
- Just to put a close to the
conversation about purpose.

00:37:43.511 --> 00:37:44.845
Yeah, I really like what Rainn was saying.

00:37:44.845 --> 00:37:47.932
My wife and I have a
couple of life mottoes,

00:37:47.932 --> 00:37:51.686
family mottoes that we focus on a lot

00:37:51.686 --> 00:37:55.398
and one of them is have
a mission, not a job.

00:37:56.274 --> 00:37:59.777
And I say this to my
students all the time.

00:37:59.777 --> 00:38:02.196
Cause I think so many of us pursue jobs.

00:38:02.196 --> 00:38:05.032
"I want to be an actor.
That's what my passion is."

00:38:05.032 --> 00:38:07.368
"I want to be a writer.
That's where my passion is."

00:38:07.368 --> 00:38:08.536
That's great.

00:38:08.536 --> 00:38:10.413
But what's your mission?

00:38:11.872 --> 00:38:14.792
Your mission is bigger than

00:38:14.792 --> 00:38:17.461
the things that you do, right.

00:38:17.461 --> 00:38:21.007
If you have a mission then
you can have multiple ways

00:38:21.007 --> 00:38:22.842
in which that mission expresses itself

00:38:22.842 --> 00:38:27.179
like acting or writing or service

00:38:27.179 --> 00:38:28.556
or whatever the thing is.

00:38:28.556 --> 00:38:31.183
So if you're sitting there and
you're thinking to yourself,

00:38:31.183 --> 00:38:33.602
"What do I want to do with my life?"

00:38:33.602 --> 00:38:35.646
Tweak that question just a little bit.

00:38:35.646 --> 00:38:37.940
What is your mission in life?

00:38:37.940 --> 00:38:41.610
Pursue that mission and I promise you,

00:38:41.610 --> 00:38:42.445
- [Rainn] Doors will open.

00:38:42.445 --> 00:38:44.405
- People will pay you for it.

00:38:44.405 --> 00:38:46.574
- But it's the rare individual who

00:38:46.574 --> 00:38:47.950
has a grip on that, right?

00:38:47.950 --> 00:38:50.036
I feel like, especially with young people,

00:38:50.036 --> 00:38:51.912
you have to live some life.

00:38:51.912 --> 00:38:54.623
You have to travel, you
have to collect experiences,

00:38:54.623 --> 00:38:56.083
You have to put yourself out there.

00:38:56.083 --> 00:38:57.585
How could you possibly...

00:38:58.878 --> 00:38:59.920
It's not a Thunderbolt.

00:38:59.920 --> 00:39:03.674
I mean, there's always the
kid who at age six knows

00:39:03.674 --> 00:39:05.134
what he or she wants to do,

00:39:05.134 --> 00:39:06.761
but that's really the outlier.

00:39:06.761 --> 00:39:09.013
- Well, this is what I
always say to, when I,

00:39:09.013 --> 00:39:11.223
cause I do some speaking at
college campuses and stuff too,

00:39:11.223 --> 00:39:14.894
and I just tell people,
"Twenties are a waste of time."

00:39:14.894 --> 00:39:15.728
Don't even worry about it.

00:39:15.728 --> 00:39:17.605
Don't try and get it figured out.

00:39:17.605 --> 00:39:20.858
The point of your twenties
is to try 12 different things

00:39:20.858 --> 00:39:24.820
and fail at nine of them
so that you can come out.

00:39:24.820 --> 00:39:27.698
But there's all this
societal pressure of like...

00:39:27.698 --> 00:39:29.283
That's not urination by the way.

00:39:29.283 --> 00:39:31.202
If you're hearing that in a microphone,

00:39:31.202 --> 00:39:32.703
that's Reza pouring a glass of water.

00:39:32.703 --> 00:39:34.080
- There's no proof of that.

00:39:34.914 --> 00:39:37.917
- But truthfully in society right now,

00:39:37.917 --> 00:39:39.418
you talk to so many college kids

00:39:39.418 --> 00:39:42.213
and they're so depressed
at 2021 cause they don't...

00:39:42.213 --> 00:39:44.590
They haven't gotten the perfect
internship over the summer

00:39:44.590 --> 00:39:47.843
and they're not pre-enrolled
in the perfect grad program

00:39:47.843 --> 00:39:51.555
and they don't have their job aligned.

00:39:51.555 --> 00:39:52.973
Now I know it's hard to
make a living out there.

00:39:52.973 --> 00:39:55.017
It's hard to have a
career and make a living.

00:39:55.017 --> 00:39:57.228
It's much harder than in
the eighties and nineties

00:39:57.228 --> 00:39:59.563
when we were getting our educations

00:39:59.563 --> 00:40:02.858
but nonetheless, if you view the twenties

00:40:02.858 --> 00:40:04.568
as a workshop stage,

00:40:04.568 --> 00:40:07.154
then it gives you some, so
you can relax a little bit,

00:40:07.154 --> 00:40:09.532
- But you gotta do some
counter-programming around that.

00:40:09.532 --> 00:40:11.951
You're asking young people
to step off the hamster wheel

00:40:11.951 --> 00:40:14.912
and that's pretty scary,
especially for a kid

00:40:14.912 --> 00:40:18.165
who's been on that track where it's like,

00:40:18.165 --> 00:40:20.334
"Get into the right
college, get the right job"

00:40:20.334 --> 00:40:22.503
And then, "Oh, if I opt out of that

00:40:22.503 --> 00:40:24.213
then life's gonna pass me by."

00:40:24.213 --> 00:40:28.634
But obviously it's quite the contrary.

00:40:28.634 --> 00:40:29.718
- And I think too for mission,

00:40:29.718 --> 00:40:31.804
and we've been on this
topic too long, but-

00:40:31.804 --> 00:40:34.598
- Yeah, we're gonna have
to pick another thing.

00:40:34.598 --> 00:40:37.476
Here you go, Reza. I just
think in terms of mission too,

00:40:37.476 --> 00:40:39.437
It doesn't have to be grand.

00:40:39.437 --> 00:40:40.729
We don't have to change the world.

00:40:40.729 --> 00:40:41.689
Your mission can be,

00:40:41.689 --> 00:40:44.358
"I want to build beautiful
things out of wood

00:40:44.358 --> 00:40:46.277
and I want to have a nice family."

00:40:46.277 --> 00:40:48.529
- That's exactly what I
mean. That's what I mean.

00:40:48.529 --> 00:40:51.073
I don't mean like, yes,
maybe your mission is to

00:40:51.073 --> 00:40:53.075
change the world. Great.

00:40:53.075 --> 00:40:54.952
Okay. Well done Greta.

00:40:57.413 --> 00:41:02.376
My mission from a very early age was to

00:41:02.835 --> 00:41:06.422
change the way that people
think through stories.

00:41:07.381 --> 00:41:10.134
I didn't say to myself,
"I'm wanna be a writer."

00:41:10.134 --> 00:41:12.219
I'm gonna be television producer.

00:41:12.219 --> 00:41:14.722
I'm gonna be a podcast host,"

00:41:14.722 --> 00:41:18.517
but all of those are just
different ways of telling stories

00:41:18.517 --> 00:41:20.686
to change the way that people think.

00:41:20.686 --> 00:41:23.481
So those are the jobs that I do,

00:41:23.481 --> 00:41:26.066
but the mission is something else.

00:41:26.066 --> 00:41:26.984
- [Rainn] That's great.

00:41:26.984 --> 00:41:27.818
- All right. My turn, My turn, My turn.

00:41:27.818 --> 00:41:29.904
Here we go. Here we go. All right.

00:41:31.614 --> 00:41:32.948
What do we got here? Okay.

00:41:34.283 --> 00:41:37.786
Oh, He he he. This is right up my alley.

00:41:37.786 --> 00:41:41.373
What do people misunderstand
about spirituality?

00:41:41.373 --> 00:41:42.666
- Ooh, that's a good one.

00:41:42.666 --> 00:41:44.168
- We're not gonna get past
this question, are we?

00:41:44.168 --> 00:41:45.878
- We just spend the next four
hours talking about this.

00:41:45.878 --> 00:41:48.088
- This is it right here. All right.

00:41:48.088 --> 00:41:49.465
Rainn and I have a lot to say about this.

00:41:49.465 --> 00:41:50.299
Let me start.

00:41:52.092 --> 00:41:53.177
First of all,

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:58.224
that religion and faith
are two different things.

00:41:58.224 --> 00:41:59.892
They're not the same thing.

00:41:59.892 --> 00:42:02.228
I think in a lot of thinking,

00:42:02.228 --> 00:42:05.481
people just assume that
that they're the same.

00:42:05.481 --> 00:42:08.108
In fact, the way that they talk, right?

00:42:08.108 --> 00:42:11.820
They'll say things like, "I
believe in Christianity" or

00:42:11.820 --> 00:42:14.448
"I believe in the Bible."

00:42:14.448 --> 00:42:16.534
And I'm like, "You believe it exists?"

00:42:16.534 --> 00:42:18.118
I don't understand what you mean by that

00:42:18.118 --> 00:42:22.122
because these are not ends

00:42:22.122 --> 00:42:22.957
in themselves,

00:42:22.957 --> 00:42:25.292
their paths to an end.

00:42:25.292 --> 00:42:28.963
Religion, spirituality,
whatever that means.

00:42:28.963 --> 00:42:30.214
Faith, whatever that means.

00:42:30.214 --> 00:42:34.343
It's individualistic, it's
nebulous, it's ineffable,

00:42:34.343 --> 00:42:38.264
it's undefinable, it's mysterious.

00:42:38.264 --> 00:42:40.766
It's experiential more than anything else.

00:42:40.766 --> 00:42:44.395
It's about how you see yourself

00:42:44.395 --> 00:42:48.065
and your place in the universe.

00:42:49.567 --> 00:42:53.362
Religion is the language that you use

00:42:53.362 --> 00:42:56.574
to express that feeling. Right?

00:42:56.574 --> 00:43:01.287
We have ways of talking about
so many of our emotions.

00:43:01.287 --> 00:43:05.666
We have an entire language
dedicated to expressing love.

00:43:05.666 --> 00:43:09.795
We have an entire language
dedicated to expressing anger.

00:43:10.838 --> 00:43:15.134
What is the language to express
this mysterious, ineffable

00:43:15.134 --> 00:43:17.344
part of human nature?

00:43:17.344 --> 00:43:21.432
Well, it's religion and that's all it is.

00:43:21.432 --> 00:43:25.519
And the problem that I think
so many people get into

00:43:25.519 --> 00:43:28.272
is that they forget that their religion,

00:43:28.272 --> 00:43:32.985
whatever their religion,
is a means to an end.

00:43:32.985 --> 00:43:37.239
They confuse it for the
end, in and of itself.

00:43:38.073 --> 00:43:41.744
And so, I think that's the biggest,

00:43:41.744 --> 00:43:43.203
to me, that's the biggest problem.

00:43:43.203 --> 00:43:46.040
So when people talk about spirituality,

00:43:46.040 --> 00:43:48.626
especially people who
claim to not be spiritual.

00:43:49.668 --> 00:43:51.795
What they really mean
is I'm not religious.

00:43:51.795 --> 00:43:54.048
That's what they really mean.

00:43:54.048 --> 00:43:58.135
When you start to poke
at them and ask and probe

00:43:58.135 --> 00:44:00.554
and try to figure out what
do they mean when they say,

00:44:00.554 --> 00:44:04.892
"I don't believe in God or
I don't accept religion."

00:44:04.892 --> 00:44:07.269
What are they actually talking about?

00:44:07.269 --> 00:44:09.271
You start to recognize that.

00:44:09.271 --> 00:44:10.939
Yeah, they're not talking
about spirituality.

00:44:10.939 --> 00:44:12.399
They're not talking about faith.

00:44:12.399 --> 00:44:17.404
They're talking about rules
and rituals and belief systems

00:44:17.863 --> 00:44:21.075
and dogmas and do's and don'ts

00:44:21.075 --> 00:44:24.953
and institutions and authority structures.

00:44:24.953 --> 00:44:27.247
What does any of that to do

00:44:27.247 --> 00:44:29.750
with faith and with spirituality?

00:44:29.750 --> 00:44:31.960
So that's, I would say, the first thing.

00:44:31.960 --> 00:44:34.380
The first thing is
recognizing the difference

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:35.506
between those two things.

00:44:35.506 --> 00:44:37.966
- Well said, and I
agree a hundred percent.

00:44:37.966 --> 00:44:40.511
And so many people can't

00:44:42.513 --> 00:44:43.514
differentiate the two.

00:44:43.514 --> 00:44:45.641
I remember I was doing this
movie with this director

00:44:45.641 --> 00:44:49.311
and he's like, "Oh, so
you're all into spirituality

00:44:49.311 --> 00:44:50.437
and God and stuff."

00:44:50.437 --> 00:44:53.023
And we were touring this
old church at the time.

00:44:53.023 --> 00:44:54.692
I was like, "Yeah, I believe in God

00:44:54.692 --> 00:44:57.111
and I love reading about
it and studying about it

00:44:57.111 --> 00:44:58.278
and talking about it and stuff."

00:44:58.278 --> 00:45:00.155
He's like, "Oh, I don't believe in God."

00:45:00.155 --> 00:45:02.366
And I was like, "Oh really? You don't."

00:45:02.366 --> 00:45:05.327
He was like, "Oh no, my
parents dragged me to church

00:45:05.327 --> 00:45:07.871
every day, not even once a
week, I had to go every day

00:45:07.871 --> 00:45:10.082
and sit for mass and I had to do this

00:45:10.082 --> 00:45:11.417
and they made me a choir boy.

00:45:11.417 --> 00:45:13.961
Oh, I don't believe in God."

00:45:13.961 --> 00:45:17.381
So it had been completely fused with his

00:45:17.381 --> 00:45:19.717
traumatic childhood experience.

00:45:19.717 --> 00:45:20.759
The fact that, you know,

00:45:20.759 --> 00:45:22.886
is there a creative force in the universe

00:45:22.886 --> 00:45:26.515
that transcends the material,

00:45:26.515 --> 00:45:28.851
has been kind of locked in,

00:45:30.018 --> 00:45:32.730
zipped in with that childhood experience.

00:45:32.730 --> 00:45:37.109
But I think that two
things come to mind for me.

00:45:37.109 --> 00:45:39.695
What do people misunderstand
about spirituality?

00:45:39.695 --> 00:45:41.196
One is that

00:45:42.364 --> 00:45:45.784
in the Hindu concept of Maya or illusion,

00:45:45.784 --> 00:45:50.414
it's that not only is it
that the material world

00:45:50.414 --> 00:45:52.624
is an illusory world.

00:45:52.624 --> 00:45:54.168
It's not even that so much,

00:45:54.168 --> 00:45:57.087
people misunderstand that,
from my limited understanding

00:45:57.087 --> 00:45:57.921
in reading about it,

00:45:57.921 --> 00:46:00.174
but this concept of Maya is that there is,

00:46:01.925 --> 00:46:06.513
we are fooled into thinking
that there is duality.

00:46:06.513 --> 00:46:08.766
So it's not that we're
fooled by the material,

00:46:08.766 --> 00:46:10.476
we're fooled into thinking
that there was duality.

00:46:10.476 --> 00:46:12.603
So I would say that

00:46:12.603 --> 00:46:15.564
this constant battle of
like religion versus science

00:46:15.564 --> 00:46:16.690
or spirituality versus science,

00:46:16.690 --> 00:46:18.901
that they're the same thing.

00:46:18.901 --> 00:46:20.611
There is one reality.

00:46:20.611 --> 00:46:24.698
There's two ways of kind
of measuring, quantifying,

00:46:24.698 --> 00:46:27.910
looking at, interacting with this reality.

00:46:27.910 --> 00:46:32.498
Science is an incredible
way of repeating experiments

00:46:32.498 --> 00:46:35.000
and understanding the material world

00:46:35.000 --> 00:46:37.878
and spirituality is again,

00:46:37.878 --> 00:46:41.131
it's the science of
understanding the other aspects

00:46:41.131 --> 00:46:42.174
of being alive.

00:46:42.174 --> 00:46:44.927
About love and service and kindness

00:46:44.927 --> 00:46:46.512
and our journeys and mission,

00:46:46.512 --> 00:46:49.973
And that they're both are harmonious.

00:46:49.973 --> 00:46:51.725
There is not a false duality.

00:46:51.725 --> 00:46:54.061
There's not a Maya,
there's not an illusion of

00:46:54.061 --> 00:46:55.479
this versus that.

00:46:55.479 --> 00:46:57.898
We're very mind, body, spirit. Yeah.

00:46:57.898 --> 00:46:59.983
- I was going to say that
the way that I refer to it,

00:46:59.983 --> 00:47:02.611
there are two modes of knowing.

00:47:02.611 --> 00:47:04.279
There are two modes of knowing.

00:47:04.279 --> 00:47:05.989
That's what they are.

00:47:05.989 --> 00:47:07.658
- Yeah, that's perfectly said.

00:47:07.658 --> 00:47:08.951
I think the other thing that people

00:47:08.951 --> 00:47:11.245
misunderstand about spirituality is,

00:47:11.245 --> 00:47:14.164
I really like nuts and bolts
practical spirituality.

00:47:15.040 --> 00:47:17.501
I don't think the spirituality has to be

00:47:17.501 --> 00:47:18.585
really airy fairy.

00:47:18.585 --> 00:47:22.798
It doesn't have to be
really like this nebulous

00:47:22.798 --> 00:47:25.509
feeling of connectivity,
although that's nice.

00:47:25.509 --> 00:47:27.427
There's nothing wrong with that.

00:47:27.427 --> 00:47:31.849
There are spiritual tools that
can make our lives better,

00:47:31.849 --> 00:47:35.310
that can actually make us
happier and more fulfilled.

00:47:35.310 --> 00:47:36.520
So there are...

00:47:36.520 --> 00:47:37.980
And those are found in
all the faith traditions.

00:47:37.980 --> 00:47:40.691
They're found in Christianity,
Judaism, Buddhism-

00:47:40.691 --> 00:47:43.402
- So that's a corner stone of Baha'i.

00:47:43.402 --> 00:47:47.322
This idea that there's a
universality of all these

00:47:47.322 --> 00:47:49.575
strains of faith.

00:47:49.575 --> 00:47:51.243
- Yeah. So the Baha'is believe that

00:47:51.243 --> 00:47:54.246
all of the world's religions
come from the same source

00:47:54.246 --> 00:47:57.499
and this God has chosen
to educate humanity

00:47:57.499 --> 00:48:00.669
by sending down these divine
teachers throughout time.

00:48:00.669 --> 00:48:02.170
We know many of them.

00:48:02.170 --> 00:48:04.381
Zorro, Aster, Abraham.

00:48:04.381 --> 00:48:06.925
To go way back, Krishna.

00:48:06.925 --> 00:48:10.220
But the Buddha, Jesus,
Muhammad, and now Baháʼu'lláh

00:48:10.220 --> 00:48:13.307
is the most recent of
these divine teachers.

00:48:13.307 --> 00:48:17.144
And they all, it's like
they're teachers in a school,

00:48:17.144 --> 00:48:20.397
the school has a purpose, which
is to educate its students.

00:48:20.397 --> 00:48:21.899
But you go have to go through kindergarten

00:48:21.899 --> 00:48:23.317
and then first grade, third grade...

00:48:23.317 --> 00:48:25.027
The teachers all know the same amount.

00:48:25.027 --> 00:48:26.111
They're all just as...

00:48:28.113 --> 00:48:31.074
What? Professional or just as wise.

00:48:31.074 --> 00:48:32.659
They have the same amount of knowing,

00:48:32.659 --> 00:48:37.664
but they're just slowly unfolding
this spiritual maturation

00:48:38.165 --> 00:48:39.958
of the human species so that

00:48:39.958 --> 00:48:42.836
these spiritual tools can
be found throughout history.

00:48:42.836 --> 00:48:44.588
And they are there to
make our lives better,

00:48:44.588 --> 00:48:47.883
Both personally and also societally.

00:48:47.883 --> 00:48:48.717
- [Rich] Yeah.

00:48:48.717 --> 00:48:51.136
- I was just gonna, just
add one more thing to this

00:48:51.136 --> 00:48:54.097
and excuse me, I'm just gonna
get a little academic here

00:48:54.097 --> 00:48:56.058
because I think it's important.

00:48:57.601 --> 00:48:59.811
What we call religion

00:48:59.811 --> 00:49:04.358
we could probably trace maybe

00:49:05.943 --> 00:49:08.779
6, 7, 8, 9,000 years,

00:49:08.779 --> 00:49:11.490
what we refer to as
institutionalized religion.

00:49:11.490 --> 00:49:13.075
What I mean by that is,

00:49:13.075 --> 00:49:16.453
it's a cohesive system
of beliefs and practices.

00:49:16.453 --> 00:49:21.375
It's a top-down system.
It's institutionalized.

00:49:21.375 --> 00:49:23.752
There's a priesthood or
whatever the case may be.

00:49:23.752 --> 00:49:25.045
- Books with rules and teachings.

00:49:25.045 --> 00:49:27.798
- Yeah. Well, even before
there was any writing.

00:49:27.798 --> 00:49:32.094
So writing was invented, maybe 3000 BC

00:49:32.094 --> 00:49:33.553
and what we call religion,

00:49:34.638 --> 00:49:39.142
we can go maybe to like 10,000, 12,000 BC.

00:49:39.142 --> 00:49:41.478
So that's the oldest temple that we have

00:49:41.478 --> 00:49:43.855
is around that period.

00:49:43.855 --> 00:49:45.983
Between 12 and 10,000 BC,

00:49:45.983 --> 00:49:47.818
the oldest temple that we have found.

00:49:48.777 --> 00:49:51.238
What we call spirituality,

00:49:51.238 --> 00:49:54.533
and I think Rainn hit it
on the head when he said

00:49:54.533 --> 00:49:56.034
what we mean by spirituality,

00:49:56.034 --> 00:50:00.038
which is recognition and communion with

00:50:00.038 --> 00:50:03.458
the thing that is beyond
the material realm.

00:50:03.458 --> 00:50:07.254
So this urge for transcendence,

00:50:07.254 --> 00:50:08.714
that which is beyond.

00:50:10.132 --> 00:50:14.678
We have hard archeological evidence

00:50:14.678 --> 00:50:16.972
for the belief

00:50:16.972 --> 00:50:19.850
in the non-material world

00:50:19.850 --> 00:50:24.855
that goes back 200, 300,000 years.

00:50:25.689 --> 00:50:30.485
We have material evidence of that belief

00:50:31.653 --> 00:50:34.740
in Neanderthals.

00:50:34.740 --> 00:50:35.699
- Not even the human species.

00:50:35.699 --> 00:50:38.118
- Not even in homo sapiens, exactly.

00:50:38.118 --> 00:50:41.663
We can trace that belief
to a hundred thousand years

00:50:41.663 --> 00:50:44.958
before there was such a
thing as homo sapiens.

00:50:44.958 --> 00:50:46.668
And then I would say even further,

00:50:46.668 --> 00:50:48.962
there's some material evidence

00:50:48.962 --> 00:50:50.797
that's a little bit more debatable

00:50:50.797 --> 00:50:52.966
that goes back all the way to Cro-Magnon.

00:50:54.176 --> 00:50:57.554
Over the last 30, 40 years or so,

00:50:57.554 --> 00:50:59.806
there's been this kind of new science

00:50:59.806 --> 00:51:01.641
in the study of religion

00:51:01.641 --> 00:51:04.728
and it's called the
Cognitive Theory of Religion.

00:51:04.728 --> 00:51:08.565
A lot of these great cognitive theorist

00:51:08.565 --> 00:51:11.151
is people who study the
way that the brain works

00:51:11.151 --> 00:51:13.320
have started to ask themselves,

00:51:13.320 --> 00:51:17.407
"Why is spirituality,"
or the religious impulse

00:51:17.407 --> 00:51:18.742
as I like to call it,

00:51:18.742 --> 00:51:21.995
"why is that a universal phenomenon?"

00:51:21.995 --> 00:51:26.792
Why is it something that
exists in every culture,

00:51:26.792 --> 00:51:30.212
in every people, in
every part of the world

00:51:30.212 --> 00:51:33.090
for all of time?

00:51:33.090 --> 00:51:37.803
Why is it something that
predates our species? Why?

00:51:37.803 --> 00:51:40.680
Because if it predates our species,

00:51:40.680 --> 00:51:43.975
if it's universal then there must be some

00:51:43.975 --> 00:51:46.812
evolutionary reason for it, right?

00:51:46.812 --> 00:51:51.525
There's gotta be some kind
of adaptive advantage to it.

00:51:53.693 --> 00:51:57.072
Long story short, there is
no adaptive advantage to it.

00:51:57.072 --> 00:51:59.658
We've asked every question
that we could possibly ask.

00:51:59.658 --> 00:52:02.536
"Well, maybe it's about
morality." "No, that's not it."

00:52:02.536 --> 00:52:06.414
"Maybe it's about answering
questions." "That's not it."

00:52:06.414 --> 00:52:09.292
"Maybe it's about social
cohesion." "Nope, that's not it."

00:52:09.292 --> 00:52:11.962
We've answered all of those questions

00:52:11.962 --> 00:52:15.423
and the fact of the matter
is that we don't know.

00:52:15.423 --> 00:52:20.303
We have no idea why, what
we call spirituality,

00:52:20.303 --> 00:52:23.348
is a universal phenomenon.

00:52:23.348 --> 00:52:25.809
Part of our evolution as a species.

00:52:25.809 --> 00:52:27.686
What is the purpose of it? We don't know.

00:52:27.686 --> 00:52:31.565
But the best answer that
the cognitive scientists

00:52:31.565 --> 00:52:36.486
that I look to, the best answer
that they've given is that

00:52:36.486 --> 00:52:37.320
it's an accident.

00:52:37.320 --> 00:52:42.325
It's just a by-product of
some other adaptive advantage

00:52:42.617 --> 00:52:46.538
that was necessary very
early in our evolution.

00:52:46.538 --> 00:52:48.999
Something that helped us survive

00:52:48.999 --> 00:52:53.003
and as a accidental echo of that thing

00:52:53.003 --> 00:52:58.008
came the universal
conception of spirituality.

00:52:58.425 --> 00:52:59.801
That's a good effort.
- It's super interesting.

00:52:59.801 --> 00:53:01.928
I would suspect that part of,

00:53:01.928 --> 00:53:06.308
would it not be considered
an evolutionary advantage

00:53:06.308 --> 00:53:09.728
to, part of this being this
quest to understand things

00:53:09.728 --> 00:53:11.646
that we don't understand, right?

00:53:11.646 --> 00:53:12.939
We look up to the stars.

00:53:12.939 --> 00:53:16.276
We don't have the scientific
tools or the acumen to really

00:53:16.276 --> 00:53:18.028
understand what that is. We create myths.

00:53:18.028 --> 00:53:19.863
We create stories around that,

00:53:19.863 --> 00:53:22.574
that help us feel better about who we are,

00:53:22.574 --> 00:53:25.327
feel like our lives have
some level of meaning.

00:53:25.327 --> 00:53:29.497
And in modern times God is dead.

00:53:29.497 --> 00:53:33.126
Spirituality, religion has
been supplanted by science.

00:53:33.126 --> 00:53:35.045
Science has become our God.

00:53:35.045 --> 00:53:37.714
Science is what we look to
for all of these answers.

00:53:37.714 --> 00:53:41.635
And there's a hubris
within the human being that

00:53:41.635 --> 00:53:45.597
science will ultimately, if
we keep doubling down on it,

00:53:45.597 --> 00:53:47.474
provide everything that we need,

00:53:47.474 --> 00:53:51.311
which now makes spirituality and religion

00:53:51.311 --> 00:53:53.355
an antiquated idea altogether.

00:53:54.231 --> 00:53:55.315
And yet it prevails.

00:53:55.315 --> 00:53:58.193
- That was one of the first theories about

00:53:58.193 --> 00:54:02.906
why the universal phenomenon
of the religious impulse.

00:54:02.906 --> 00:54:05.408
The problem with that theory
is that there's literally

00:54:05.408 --> 00:54:07.661
no adaptive advantage, whatsoever,

00:54:07.661 --> 00:54:11.164
to having airy fairy answers

00:54:11.164 --> 00:54:14.251
to the questions of the universe.

00:54:14.251 --> 00:54:15.627
Quite the contrary.

00:54:15.627 --> 00:54:18.588
In fact, what those answers do,

00:54:18.588 --> 00:54:20.465
"Why does the sun shine?"

00:54:20.465 --> 00:54:23.426
"Because there's a sun
God," et cetera. All that.

00:54:23.426 --> 00:54:25.679
What those answers end up doing

00:54:25.679 --> 00:54:30.684
is forcing individuals to
exert resources and energy

00:54:31.142 --> 00:54:33.937
that should be spent on survival.

00:54:33.937 --> 00:54:38.316
So it's actually the opposite
of an adaptive advantage.

00:54:38.316 --> 00:54:40.652
- Because if you're
burying your Viking king

00:54:40.652 --> 00:54:43.863
and you put your swords
and jewelry in the tomb

00:54:43.863 --> 00:54:46.324
because he's gonna need them to go

00:54:46.324 --> 00:54:48.994
on his quest into Valhalla,

00:54:48.994 --> 00:54:53.581
then you've done a great
disservice to your community.

00:54:53.581 --> 00:54:56.042
- Absolutely. I mean,
we don't need to get too

00:54:56.042 --> 00:54:57.043
deeply involved in this,

00:54:57.043 --> 00:54:58.962
but I mean, I'm sure a lot
of people out there are like,

00:54:58.962 --> 00:55:00.839
"Well, what about this
and what about that?"

00:55:00.839 --> 00:55:02.507
"What about social cohesion?"

00:55:02.507 --> 00:55:05.218
"Doesn't religious belief or spirituality

00:55:05.218 --> 00:55:07.470
help a community become tighter?

00:55:07.470 --> 00:55:09.306
And so if a community is tighter

00:55:09.306 --> 00:55:11.224
they have a better chance of survival?"

00:55:11.224 --> 00:55:14.894
True, except that that's
not how Neanderthals

00:55:14.894 --> 00:55:17.230
or homo sapiens created community.

00:55:17.230 --> 00:55:18.732
Community was kinship.

00:55:19.691 --> 00:55:21.067
You didn't create your community

00:55:21.067 --> 00:55:22.610
because you all believe the same thing.

00:55:22.610 --> 00:55:24.195
You created your community because you

00:55:24.195 --> 00:55:25.822
shared the same blood.

00:55:25.822 --> 00:55:28.116
- Honestly, that didn't really
happen until Christianity.

00:55:28.116 --> 00:55:30.368
- Or your survival depends on it.

00:55:30.368 --> 00:55:32.829
- But here's the bottom line
of what I'm trying to say

00:55:32.829 --> 00:55:35.373
here is that we don't know.

00:55:35.373 --> 00:55:36.958
Maybe we'll find out one day.

00:55:36.958 --> 00:55:40.545
We don't know why religion, or I'm sorry,

00:55:40.545 --> 00:55:44.382
why the religious impulse,
the desire for transcendence,

00:55:44.382 --> 00:55:46.384
the belief that there is something else

00:55:46.384 --> 00:55:49.596
besides the material realm,
why is that universal?

00:55:49.596 --> 00:55:53.016
Why is it part of our evolutionary
process? We don't know.

00:55:53.016 --> 00:55:55.977
But embedded in the question, to me,

00:55:55.977 --> 00:55:57.228
is the answer.

00:55:58.438 --> 00:56:00.690
Embedded in the question is the fact that

00:56:00.690 --> 00:56:03.443
whatever spirituality is, okay,

00:56:03.443 --> 00:56:05.070
back to the question,

00:56:05.070 --> 00:56:07.030
what do we misunderstand
about spirituality?

00:56:07.030 --> 00:56:08.948
Whatever it is,

00:56:08.948 --> 00:56:12.869
it is the normal
functioning of your brain.

00:56:14.037 --> 00:56:15.163
That's what it is.

00:56:15.163 --> 00:56:20.168
Your brain is designed to think

00:56:21.252 --> 00:56:25.340
that there is a reality
beyond the material realm.

00:56:25.340 --> 00:56:27.717
- And we've been thinking this
way for a 100, 200,000 years.

00:56:27.717 --> 00:56:30.261
- Yes. So you can deny
that if you want to.

00:56:30.261 --> 00:56:31.679
You can say, "No, there isn't anything

00:56:31.679 --> 00:56:33.681
beyond the material realm." Fine.

00:56:33.681 --> 00:56:34.933
But that's a conscious decision.

00:56:34.933 --> 00:56:37.811
Your brain is designed
to think that there is.

00:56:37.811 --> 00:56:40.772
And maybe we should take
that seriously for a minute.

00:56:40.772 --> 00:56:43.983
Yeah, We can talk about
why. We don't know.

00:56:43.983 --> 00:56:45.610
But the fact that it does,

00:56:45.610 --> 00:56:50.365
the fact that your brain
is meant to think this way,

00:56:50.365 --> 00:56:51.866
should make us stop and think,

00:56:51.866 --> 00:56:54.661
"Well, maybe there's something to it."

00:56:54.661 --> 00:56:56.496
- It's Fantastic. I thought
you said you were tired today.

00:56:56.496 --> 00:56:58.123
- [Rich] Yeah. I know. You
said you were out of it.

00:56:58.123 --> 00:56:59.707
- Then somebody brought up spirituality.

00:56:59.707 --> 00:57:02.043
(all chatter)

00:57:02.043 --> 00:57:04.087
- Before you do that, though, I wanted to

00:57:05.213 --> 00:57:07.674
follow up on one point
that you made Rainn about

00:57:08.883 --> 00:57:10.009
dualistic thinking.

00:57:10.009 --> 00:57:13.054
Reza you said many people will say,

00:57:15.432 --> 00:57:18.268
"I'm not spiritual" because
they're affiliating that

00:57:18.268 --> 00:57:19.644
with religion.

00:57:21.020 --> 00:57:23.064
I think in Los Angeles, it's the inverse.

00:57:23.064 --> 00:57:25.733
People will say they're spiritual
but they're not religious.

00:57:25.733 --> 00:57:29.863
But on that idea of binary thinking that

00:57:29.863 --> 00:57:32.490
science is at the
exclusion of spirituality.

00:57:32.490 --> 00:57:33.700
I mean, for me,

00:57:35.118 --> 00:57:38.204
the more you delve into spirituality,

00:57:38.204 --> 00:57:40.707
the more amazing everything becomes

00:57:40.707 --> 00:57:43.001
and the deeper you dive into science,

00:57:43.001 --> 00:57:45.044
the more mystical it becomes.

00:57:45.044 --> 00:57:48.590
I don't see those as being
mutually exclusive pursuits.

00:57:48.590 --> 00:57:52.218
You can be rational and science minded

00:57:52.218 --> 00:57:57.140
and insist on evidence for your beliefs

00:57:57.140 --> 00:58:00.435
and be profoundly, deeply spiritual.

00:58:00.435 --> 00:58:02.270
- Yeah. Rainn and I, We were just,

00:58:02.270 --> 00:58:03.646
we actually just were talking about this

00:58:03.646 --> 00:58:04.981
on a recent episode.

00:58:07.692 --> 00:58:08.485
The foundation of...

00:58:11.779 --> 00:58:13.406
Let me put it this way.

00:58:13.406 --> 00:58:16.701
The fundamental law of physics is

00:58:16.701 --> 00:58:19.954
the preservation of energy and matter.

00:58:19.954 --> 00:58:23.208
The belief that everything
that exists today

00:58:23.208 --> 00:58:27.420
has always existed and will always exist

00:58:27.420 --> 00:58:29.797
as long as the universe exists.

00:58:29.797 --> 00:58:33.051
Man, Sufi's have been saying that

00:58:33.051 --> 00:58:34.677
for 1500 years.

00:58:36.095 --> 00:58:39.432
You're right. The more we
know about the universe,

00:58:39.432 --> 00:58:42.143
the more science advances,

00:58:42.143 --> 00:58:44.312
the more spiritual it starts to sound.

00:58:44.312 --> 00:58:45.605
You're absolutely right.

00:58:46.606 --> 00:58:47.607
- All right. Let's see what we got here.

00:58:47.607 --> 00:58:48.900
- See what you got.

00:58:51.361 --> 00:58:52.487
- I'm gonna pick the dud.

00:58:52.487 --> 00:58:54.239
(Reza laugh)

00:58:54.239 --> 00:58:55.949
- See that self-defeating
attitude right there,

00:58:55.949 --> 00:58:57.200
What's up with that?

00:58:57.200 --> 00:58:58.535
- Oh, well. I didn't pick a dud,

00:58:58.535 --> 00:58:59.786
but I picked what we just talked about.

00:58:59.786 --> 00:59:02.580
"What is faith and how does
it differ from religion?"

00:59:02.580 --> 00:59:05.250
And we already traversed
that a little bit so

00:59:05.250 --> 00:59:06.918
let's go on to something else.

00:59:14.259 --> 00:59:17.262
"Can you be rational science
and still spiritual?"

00:59:17.262 --> 00:59:18.096
We covered that.

00:59:18.096 --> 00:59:20.390
- Okay.
- Yeah we pretty good at this.

00:59:20.390 --> 00:59:21.683
You know what, we can
just ask the question

00:59:21.683 --> 00:59:24.227
and splice it in the answers.

00:59:24.227 --> 00:59:25.061
- Let's see.

00:59:26.938 --> 00:59:28.648
How do you be a good person?

00:59:28.648 --> 00:59:29.857
- Ha ha.

00:59:29.857 --> 00:59:31.484
- We touched on that a
little bit with Mike Schur

00:59:31.484 --> 00:59:33.152
but we'd be happy to dig in a little more.

00:59:33.152 --> 00:59:34.904
- Maybe elaborate a little bit on that

00:59:34.904 --> 00:59:38.366
and then we can move on
quickly to another one.

00:59:38.366 --> 00:59:41.619
If there's more to say-
- What do you think, Rich?

00:59:41.619 --> 00:59:43.580
Have you been a good person?
- I don't...

00:59:43.580 --> 00:59:46.207
I can't say I have the
ultimate answer to that.

00:59:46.207 --> 00:59:50.128
I think that there is wisdom in

00:59:53.673 --> 00:59:58.678
philosophical and spiritual
strains of thought

00:59:58.761 --> 01:00:01.139
that have survived for thousands of years,

01:00:01.139 --> 01:00:03.474
from which you can extract principles

01:00:03.474 --> 01:00:05.977
for how to orient your life.

01:00:05.977 --> 01:00:08.813
Fundamentally, I think
that being a good person

01:00:10.106 --> 01:00:13.359
is intertwined with
that search for purpose

01:00:13.359 --> 01:00:15.069
and it always goes back to

01:00:16.154 --> 01:00:19.073
finding a way to devote that purpose

01:00:19.073 --> 01:00:20.408
in service to other people.

01:00:20.408 --> 01:00:25.413
I think fundamentally, being
good is really about service.

01:00:25.413 --> 01:00:29.375
- Yeah. Yeah. I heard this
phrase the other day about

01:00:30.585 --> 01:00:31.711
being otherish.

01:00:31.711 --> 01:00:33.796
Someone was talking about it,
I forget who was saying that.

01:00:33.796 --> 01:00:38.384
We're selfish and human
beings are naturally selfish

01:00:38.384 --> 01:00:39.636
for a number of reasons, I think.

01:00:39.636 --> 01:00:42.805
Because children need to
be selfish to survive.

01:00:42.805 --> 01:00:45.350
We needed, as a species,
to be selfish to survive.

01:00:45.350 --> 01:00:49.228
I'm gonna feed myself this deer carcass

01:00:49.228 --> 01:00:50.688
and make sure that the people in my cave

01:00:50.688 --> 01:00:52.231
get the deer carcass.

01:00:52.231 --> 01:00:54.901
And then my children get the
piece of the deer carcass.

01:00:54.901 --> 01:00:55.985
And there's a,

01:00:55.985 --> 01:00:58.071
I sounded like Dwight Schrute just then,

01:00:58.071 --> 01:01:00.406
but there's a selfishness in this.

01:01:00.406 --> 01:01:03.910
But then as we grow and
mature and progress,

01:01:03.910 --> 01:01:08.289
then we can be otherish
instead of selfish.

01:01:08.289 --> 01:01:11.042
And how do we be a good person? We just...

01:01:11.042 --> 01:01:14.879
When I find myself being good
and I haven't always been good

01:01:14.879 --> 01:01:16.172
and I struggle with being good,

01:01:16.172 --> 01:01:19.884
it's not like I'm arrived by
any stretch of the imagination.

01:01:19.884 --> 01:01:20.718
Ask my wife.

01:01:21.678 --> 01:01:25.723
But the idea of trying to be
otherish instead of selfish

01:01:26.683 --> 01:01:28.559
helps me in that path.

01:01:28.559 --> 01:01:30.770
- I like that term otherish.
I hadn't heard that before.

01:01:30.770 --> 01:01:31.938
- I'm gonna steal that.

01:01:31.938 --> 01:01:33.356
- [Rainn] Good. You're welcome to it.

01:01:33.356 --> 01:01:35.316
- All right. Pick a new one.

01:01:35.316 --> 01:01:36.150
I think you're up.

01:01:36.150 --> 01:01:37.443
- It's up to me now.

01:01:37.443 --> 01:01:38.653
- [Reza] Here we go.

01:01:38.653 --> 01:01:40.405
- There's a lot of gabbing.

01:01:40.405 --> 01:01:41.948
"What happens after we die?"

